#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# discrete_shift

#% text
# `discrete_shift(f, k)` gives `f` with `k` replaced by `k + 1`; `discrete_shift(f, [k, m])` by `k + m`, `discrete_shift(f, [k, m, h])` by `k + m h`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Shifted values of known functions are written with the unshifted ones (`gamma(k + 3) = k (k + 1) (k + 2) gamma(k)`), with a polynomial factor expanded.

#% item
# Sums, products and limits whose bounds move with `k` shift their summand instead.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
discrete_shift(f(i), [i, 3])
#% output
#= f(3 + i)

#% cell
discrete_shift(gamma(i), [i, 3])
#% output
#= (2*i + 3*i^2 + i^3)*gamma(i)

#% subsection
# Scope

#% cell
discrete_shift(fibonacci(i), [i, 5])
#% output
#= 3*fibonacci(i) + 5*fibonacci(1 + i)

#% cell
discrete_shift(sum(f(k), [k, i + a, i + b]), i)
#% output
#= sum(f(1 + k), [k, a + i, b + i])

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# A summation variable is bound: the sum does not move:

#% cell
[discrete_shift(sum(f(i), [i, a, b]), i), discrete_shift(product(f(i), [i, m, n]), n)]
#% output
#= [sum(f(i), [i, a, b]), product(f(i), [i, m, 1 + n])]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `difference_delta`, `discrete_ratio`
